<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Whole-number points in a region</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>R is the region given by \({@D[1]@}\), \({@D[2]@}\), \({@D[3]@}\), \(x \ge 0\) and \(y \ge 0\).</p><p>(a) Give a point of R whose coordinates are whole numbers: ( [[input:ans1]] , [[input:ans2]] ) [[validation:ans1]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Give a point of R with whole-number coordinates that lies on the line \({@D[4]@}\): ( [[input:ans3]] , [[input:ans4]] ) [[validation:ans3]] [[validation:ans4]]</p><div>[[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>There is more than one right answer.</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Test a point in every inequality. For example \(({@pin[1]@}, {@pin[2]@})\) is in R: \({@a1@} \times {@pin[1]@} + {@b1@} \times {@pin[2]@} = {@a1*pin[1]+b1*pin[2]@} \le {@c1@}\), \({@a2@} \times {@pin[1]@} + {@b2@} \times {@pin[2]@} = {@a2*pin[1]+b2*pin[2]@} \gt {@c2@}\), and it satisfies \({@D[3]@}\). R has \({@np@}\) whole-number points in all.</p><p>(b) The line \({@D[4]@}\) is solid, so its points can be in R. Whole-number points of R on it: \({@P1@}\). Points on the dashed line \({@a2@}x + {@b2@}y = {@c2@}\) are not in R.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
